o  that you never miss out  all right so let's just watch a little  quick introductory  clip i'm khloe kardashian growing up  people called me the fat funny sister  until one day i started working out  eating right and putting myself first  and you know what i've never felt better  now i'm helping others transform by  hooking them up with my favorite  hollywood trainers  and glam experts to turn their lives  around and shut down the shamers  because a great body is the best revenge  yikes uh her words folks  not mine so the concept of revenge body  if you  didn't quite grasp it there was inspired  by khloe kardashian's own  personal weight loss journey and  post-divorce revenge body  she claims that her body transformation  not only allowed her to feel  better than ever but it was also a way  to  fight back against body shamers in the  media who  always identified her as the funny fat  sisters of the kardashian clan  now i can fully appreciate that that was  not easy especially in hollywood  but chloe uses her personal experience  as the backdrop  of the show to really encourage  participants to lose weight in a way to  gain revenge on their haters  so yeah she basically just took the  premise of every 90s teen movie  and threw in some adult life challenges  like infertility infidelity  and divorce as for how participants  enact  said revenge the answer is very simple  they are paired up with celebrity  trainers doctors nutritionists  glam experts dermatologists dentists  psychic mediums  you name it and after following a strict  diet and workout plan for 12 weeks  they confront the victim of their  revenge at their big reveal party  sounds pretty straightforward totally  realistic and sustainable  right well now that you know the basics  of the show  let's get into the nitty-gritty and  discuss my  top reasons why i feel the show needs to  go  reason number one weight loss is  prescribed for  all the wrong reasons now let's be real  the majority of individuals who seek  weight loss  usually do it either for health or  aesthetic reasons  both of which are totally valid goals  and one in my opinion is not more worthy  of pursuit  than the other i don't think it's  productive to discriminate against  people who have aesthetic goals for  themselves  if that is what brings them joy however  this show encourages weight loss for the  purpose of revenge  against somebody else because in the  words of khloe kardashian  the great body is the best revenge and  while the term  revenge body may sound empowering it can  actually be  very problematic and harmful for one's  relationship with their body  not only does it imply that you must  alter your physical appearance to  essentially  manipulate somebody who has hurt you by  making them feel badly  but you are also changing your body for  somebody  else rather than for your own health and  wellness goals  research has also shown that behaviors  that are rooted in revenge and seeking  punishment towards someone else  can very easily lead to obsession  meaning that a revenge based  workout plan or diet that is externally  motivated  can easily become obsessive and  potentially even disorderly  it also feeds into the idea that our  bodies are the best form of currency to  receive  love validation and respect from others  rather than focusing on more important  things  like how we treat the people around us  as well as the self  love and care and validation that we  provide towards ourselves  luckily some participants of the show  tend to have a realization  somewhere along the way that their  weight loss journey should be  for their own sake rather than for  outside justification  and approval however despite these  realizations  the underlying messaging of the show  perpetuates the idea that one must  become more physically desirable  in order to be accepted and win  not only that but by focusing  exclusively on weight loss to appease  somebody else  the show totally misses an opportunity  to teach  really important lessons about self-love  body kindness and acceptance  all of which are really really important  practices  for participants to embrace long term  now moving on to reason number two the  show  tends to focus too much on weight and  not  overall health this is where i get a  little riled up  so chloe preaches that body  transformations are not about the amount  of weight lost  but rather how you feel in your mind  body and soul  now i wholeheartedly believe that  exercise and movement  should not only feel good physically  regardless of weight loss  but it can also help provide both  emotional  and mental benefits which are just  equally nourishing  so i can more or less get on the same  page with chloe when it comes to this  belief  but that is pretty much where it ends  let's take a look you're eating a bag of  junk food  i put three in my mouth right now  because i was starving  right now because i check in with  everyone you're the one who's lost the  least amount of weight  on a program like this you should be up  a good solid  six pounds of muscle already and down at  least 12 or 15 pounds on that scale  and we are less than half of that oh  lord this is horrible  have you been really honest in your food  journal  no but um you know i thought by not  writing things down on my paper  oh he wouldn't find out he write down  the paper so you know but he's going to  find out from your blood work anyway so  i would not write things down but  the proof is in your blood it can't lie  i really do think that i'm eating good  besides the potato  and like the rice and the refried beans  we're intervening now because there's  still time to flip this around  you have the best of the best at your  fingertips take advantage of it  it's only a few weeks and it will change  your life forever  and if i don't see numbers a change in  you  then i'm just gonna have to drop you and  get somebody new  this is inhumane  so despite chloe's supposed holistic  beliefs around weight loss  participants are viciously picked apart  and pressured to lose more weight  they're even threatened to be dropped  from the show if weight loss is not  achieved  in addition when weight loss is deemed  unsuccessful or inadequate  it is essentially chalked up to a lack  of willpower  rather than looking at the myriad of  reasons why  somebody may struggle to reach their  weight loss goals like  genetics age stress medications and  underlying health conditions  not to mention the fact that often these  goals are  pretty ambitious and extreme this  mentality around weight loss being a  strict  numbers game based on willpower alone  perpetuates the false message that  achieving a good body  is the only answer to life's problems  regardless of the cost  and we know that weight loss or being in  a smaller body  is not necessarily an indicator of  health  research has shown that individuals of  normal weight  can be metabolically unfit while  overweight people  can be just as easily metabolically  healthy  weight and health are correlated in some  cases of course  but not causation by any means however  from what i've seen of the show  i don't think that achieving health is  necessarily as much of a priority anyway  as it is just to make somebody's body  smaller  and the methods to achieve smaller  bodies among participants  are pretty effing messed up for example  participants are expected to work out  five to seven days a week for up to two  hours  and sometimes multiple times a day and  while this workout routine  may arguably work for experienced  athletes and routine gym goers  for participants of the show this is  like going  from zero to a hundred  it's honestly just unsafe  don't believe me just like watch this  work come on  two go thank you i need that  let it out all right it ain't supposed  to be in there  let me tell you how important this is to  me i'm sitting here watching you throw  up i can't even look at people do that  he's not the first person i've trained  that came the first day and threw up  now you got to dig deep for real because  we got a lot we got a long way to go  having a solid workout does not need to  mean making yourself  throw up or even worse risking  serious injury like i said i am totally  all for aesthetic goals if that is what  brings a person joy  but i do not believe that achieving a  hot body or revenge body  should ever be prioritized above  somebody's physical  and emotional health and by following  this extreme and like downright  torturous  workout method it's really not  surprising that participants  generally lose up to like five pounds a  week  which is significantly higher than the  recommended one to two pounds a week  that is generally considered healthy and  sustainable for weight loss  with that said i really wish we were  seeing more emphasis on improving  healthy behaviors in a sustainable way  and less emphasis on making participants  feel like straight up  crap in order to achieve some kind of  shock  value at the reveal party for the sake  of good tv  so reason number three this show needs  to go is really that it does not  adequately address the root cause  of their weight gain and emotional  eating a lot of the participants on  revenge body identify themselves as  emotional eaters however the show just  does not  do enough to support or teach  participants  how to cope with their emotions without  using food  so for example when one participant  acknowledged  clearly that they have emotional issues  with food  i don't feel they were adequately  addressed by a professional therapy team  as they really should have  let's take a look after my parents  passed away i gained  probably like 15 or 20 pounds but i've  been an athlete in my entire life  so i like lost it and i got back into  good shape for my freshman year of  college at ucla  you know i was enjoying everything about  my life at school  and then i was  raped since then i gained close to 70  pounds  [Music]  and i'm kind of haunted by it hate is  like drinking poison and hoping the  other person dies  in order to get past it to stop with the  eating  you have to forgive okay so following  some  obvious trauma in her life this  participant named jill  explicitly told her nutritionist that  she desired a  healthy relationship with food and in  response to this  the nutritionist suggested that the only  solution to her emotional eating  and to encourage weight loss was for her  to forgive her  sexual assaulter now while this may or  may not be a valid contributor to this  person's relationship with food and may  or may not be part of healing  i don't know i'm not a therapist i would  hope  that if somebody was struggling with  trauma to the extent  where it was causing them to use food  exclusively to cope  that they would speak to a qualified  therapist to really help them  unpack and process this in a healthy way  but instead they're essentially being  told that being physically desirable  through extreme weight loss  and restriction is all that they need to  be happy  which can actually further exacerbate an  unhealthy relationship with food  and body not to mention this  recommendation feels  really out of scope for a nutrition  professional  myself included never mind an  unregulated professional  like a nutritionist and that brings me  to reason number four  this 12-week intensive weight loss  program that they are prescribing  is likely really unsustainable in the  long run  as i mentioned previously participants  of the show have access to  a plethora of professional support and  resources  during their 12-week stint on the show  so including  trainers doctors gym memberships  meal services nutritionists the full  gamut  while these resources could certainly  help expedite their weight loss albeit  maybe  not such a healthy way like we discussed  as soon as the filming  ends access to these resources also  comes to an end  and i mean sure it might be possible for  participants to keep  up the momentum of their intensive  program  assuming they want to quit their job and  put the bill for gym memberships and  nutritionists and trainers and meal  plans  but the research tells us that  sustaining significant weight loss  through  extreme dieting and exercise is  not an easy feat especially when it's  done  so rapidly quick a good example of this  is the research done on another popular  weight loss reality tv show  the biggest loser which let's be real is  really the  og in dumpster fire diet shows so in the  research on the biggest loser  researchers followed up with  participants of the show six years later  and found that their fullness hormone  leptin decreased  while their metabolic rate had also  significantly slowed down  the result was that while they  anticipated the resting metabolic rate  would be lower  at a lower body weight it had actually  declined an  additional 500 calories on top of this  that right there especially in  combination with silenced fullness  signals  makes it literally impossible to keep  weight off so it's really not surprising  that they would put the weight back  on and while i don't have any long-term  studies on chloe's  shows participants i would put money on  the fact that  they would gain it back too one of my  biggest issues with extreme weight loss  reality tv shows like the biggest loser  and revenge body is that they feed into  this idea  that weight gain and weight loss is  exclusively within the control  of the individual they also imply that  the only way that you can achieve  health and acceptance in society is by  controlling your weight  finally they tend to perpetuate societal  weight bias  by depicting weight gain as bad and  requiring modification  while always showing weight loss as  being good and something that we should  all  aspire to achieve in order to be happy  or in this case  get revenge and that is even more  cringe-worthy for reasons that i've  already expressed a minute ago  so even though the interventions and  weight loss methods implemented  on the show are completely unsustainable  and inaccessible for the majority of  people who are watching at home  if weight loss is not achieved or  maintained  participants are immediately deemed a  failure  and i really worry how the general  population will interpret that  about their own inability to have  similar success  which brings me to reason number five  contestants are given nutrition advice  from  non-nutrition professionals uh  you know i have to comment on this  so i did allude to this earlier in  reference to the scope of a nutritionist  versus  a therapist or psychologist but i want  to comment on it specifically  related to nutrition since that is my  field and it's my expertise  now even though the show's emphasis  seems to be on an extreme  exercise regime as the weight loss  method participants  also receive individualized meal plans  and diet advice  from people who are definitely not  registered dietitians  based on the research while there were  very few trainers with some  sort of nutrition certification the  majority actually held  no credentials and none of them were  registered dietitians  even if i didn't go looking for this  information for the purpose of this  review  i would know that no rds were consulted  for this show because  no rd would prescribe an all liquid diet  for weight loss  and call it safe and sustainable  let me show you what i mean this is  phase one  is five days you're gonna have a  smoothie for breakfast  a smoothie for lunch and a smoothie for  dinner and a crunchy snack between each  first five days you're gonna drop a lot  of weight you're always gonna be full  and it's not gonna be a thousand  calories it's going to be 400 calories  and it's going to have protein and fiber  and healthy fat and it's going to be  incredible  400 calories incredible  how is that safe or sustainable  how did that even make it past ethics  dietitians in contrast are held to  really high ethics standards  and use evidence to inform their  practice and recommendations i do not  know of any well-rounded literature  suggesting that an  all-liquid kind of juice cleanse diet is  a good  weight loss solution because yes you  will probably  lose weight drinking just 400 calories  worth of juice every day  but that weight loss is largely just  water weight  and you could not maintain that weight  loss momentum  for very long because you would never be  kept on a 400 calorie juice diet for  very long  i would hope at least clearly the  recommendations made from the show  are made for the sole purposes of  getting results  really really fast because obviously  rapid body transformations  make for better television but if you  surveyed all of my dietitian colleagues  i think we would all agree  that this is a highly unethical approach  to nutrition counseling  sensationalism over safety is never okay  which brings me to my last point and  it's only the last one because i'm  trying to be succinct  i legit could probably rant all day but  number six  the show promotes overly restrictive  diets  so not only are participants often  receiving dieting advice sometimes from  professionals that don't actually have a  whole lot of training  or authority in nutrition they also are  being advised to be  restrictive as f to reach their weight  loss goals  let's take a look rule of thumb  if there's more than five ingredients on  something you want to say no  this is like processed crap  really sugar is the devil it is  a fat that sugar is more addictive than  cocaine  ugh i hate this weight loss show trope  of trainer  throwing out participants food is so  insensitive to those experiencing  food security which often goes hand in  hand  with obesity it's also so holier than  thou of the trainer that i just  it honestly makes me sick to watch but  participants are  pretty much told to avoid every food in  the diet culture handbook  including gluten dairy alcohol processed  foods  toxic foods and sugar of course because  sugar  is the devil  i also already discussed the concept of  sugar addiction in detail so i'm going  to link  to that video here and also below but  once all their familiar foods are thrown  out into the bin  and a million new rules are imposed a  lot of participants are put on a  body reset where like i said they can  only drink smoothies three times a day  which according to their trainer is  a safe realistic and sustainable way to  lose  weight let's check it out what jessie  doesn't realize  is i'm giving her a safe realistic  sustainable way to lose weight and if  she doesn't appreciate this  goodbye that is pretty harsh  uh and telling someone like strictly  telling somebody  what they're going to eat not exactly  the  counseling skills that i was trained in  in school  but anyways besides the long laundry  list of foods to avoid and this whole  kind of  smoothie set up they have to start with  participants are mostly encouraged to  eat  whole foods that are grown in the ground  while i am totally all for a way  of eating that prioritizes whole foods  being  overly restrictive combined with an  intense exercise regime  can very easily leave someone feeling  physically and  emotionally deprived and if you're a  long-term viewer of abby's kitchen  you know where that's going to lead  after their 12-week stint  probably eating all the gluten dairy  alcohol processed foods  toxic foods and sugar and never wanting  to see another damp smoothie again  bottom line i'm going to give chloe the  benefit of the doubt here and assume  that she herself is coming from a  good place in wanting to improve the  lives of participants  of the show however i just don't think  that she's done her research to  really fully understand and grasp where  her well-meaned  efforts can lead participants into poor  states  of physical and emotional health while  weight loss can  absolutely be achieved in a sustainable  and healthy way  putting somebody into an extreme set of  circumstances for a limited period of  time  all for the purpose of revenge  is just a recipe for disaster  not to mention it sends all the wrong  messages to vulnerable viewers about how  weight loss  should look why it should be attempted  and  how it should be achieved so on that  note  this dietitian absolutely thinks that  this show  should be boycotted and cancelled  so folks that is all for today's video  thank you again to skillshare for  sponsoring this video  if you liked it be sure to give it the  thumbs up subscribe to the channel  and i will see you next time on abby's  kitchen bye  [Music]  you

